<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key><string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
  <key>CFBundleExecutable</key><string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
  <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key><string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
  <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key><string>6.0</string>
  <key>CFBundleName</key><string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
  <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key><string>Haven</string>
  <key>CFBundlePackageType</key><string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_PACKAGE_TYPE)</string>
  <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key><string>0.1.0</string>
  <key>CFBundleVersion</key><string>1</string>
  <key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key><true/>
  <key>UILaunchScreen</key><dict/>
  <key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key><dict>
    <key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key><true/>
  </dict>
  <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key><array>
    <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
  </array>
  <!--
    App Transport Security. `NSAllowsLocalNetworking` alone — the blanket `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`
    that sat beside it (and overrode it) is gone. Cleartext HTTP to a Home Assistant on the local
    network is a first-class configuration and stays allowed; cleartext to a public host is now
    refused by iOS rather than merely by our own convention. That is the design's §1 trust model,
    checked by the platform.

    SCOPE — what this actually covers, which is one path out of three. ATS governs URLSession (and
    WKWebView) in *this* process. So Haven's OAuth token exchange and refresh (URLSessionHTTP,
    OAuthClient) are OS-enforced. Two things are not:
      - The WebSocket. NWWebSocketConnection builds NWParameters directly and picks TLS from the
        wss:// scheme, so it is outside ATS entirely; its TLS comes from ConnectionEndpoint forcing
        https/wss on every remote candidate, enforced by HavenCore with tests.
      - The sign-in web sheet. WebAuthPresenter uses ASWebAuthenticationSession, which runs
        out-of-process and is governed by that process, not by this plist.
    Do not describe the trust model as platform-checked end to end.

    KNOWN LIMITATION, NOT YET VERIFIED ON DEVICE. ATS judges the *hostname*, not the address it
    resolves to. NSAllowsLocalNetworking covers unqualified hostnames, .local names, and
    private-range IP literals — but a fully-qualified name like hass.example.com that resolves to
    192.168.1.10 is judged a public host and would be BLOCKED over http. The concrete shape this
    takes: AppModel.signIn() fills in http:// for a scheme-less entry, so a user typing
    hass.example.com gets http://hass.example.com and, if that name is a FQDN, ATS will refuse the
    token request at runtime. This cannot be confirmed by a build — a successful build proves only
    that this file parses. If it bites real setups, the fix is a narrow NSExceptionDomains entry,
    NOT a return to NSAllowsArbitraryLoads. Tracked in the design doc §8.
  -->
  <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key><dict>
    <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key><true/>
  </dict>
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key><string>Haven connects to your Home Assistant server on your local network.</string>
  <!--
    Required by NEHotspotNetwork.fetchCurrent(), which iOS gates on Location Services. Haven asks
    for this ONLY from Settings › Connection, framed as "connect faster at home" — never during
    onboarding. It is used to compare the current Wi-Fi network's name against the one Haven last
    reached Home Assistant on locally; no location is read, stored or transmitted. Declining leaves
    the app fully functional (it probes the local address first and falls back).
  -->
  <key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key><string>Haven uses this only to see which Wi-Fi network you're on, so it can connect straight to your home instead of trying and waiting. Your location is never read, stored or shared.</string>
  <key>CFBundleURLTypes</key><array><dict>
    <key>CFBundleURLName</key><string>app.haven.HavenApp</string>
    <key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key><array><string>havenapp</string></array>
  </dict></array>
</dict></plist>
